WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.840
These are six things every high

00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:07.560
performer needs to know how to do. And I

00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:09.920
mean, you have to really do them. Not

00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:11.880
just read about them or watch videos,

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:15.720
practice doing them. We don't learn

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:17.520
about these in school or at work because

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:19.560
no one teaches them. I've lived through

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:21.800
enough lives to know which ones move the

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:24.400
needle. You know, I started as a monk in

00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:28.440
training and somehow ended up becoming

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:30.120
the CEO, board member, investor in tech

00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:32.439
and AI [music] companies. And these

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:34.120
skills have helped me find success time

00:00:32.439 --> 00:00:35.840
and time again. In this video, I'll

00:00:34.120 --> 00:00:38.360
break them down one by one. And you

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:40.720
know, in the age of AI, these skills can

00:00:38.360 --> 00:00:43.880
bring you immense wealth and a sense of

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:46.040
worth. And they make you genuinely

00:00:43.880 --> 00:00:48.200
self-reliant, no matter [music] what the

00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:50.120
world throws at you. High performance is

00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:52.640
not just one skill. It's three

00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:55.480
relationships happening all at once.

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:58.320
With yourself, with other people, and

00:00:55.480 --> 00:01:01.160
with reality. Neglect one and the other

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:03.880
two can't save you. So, we'll cover six

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:06.440
essential skills. Two for each. And one

00:01:03.880 --> 00:01:08.680
skill at the end that rules them all.

00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:10.320
So, let's get going. We always admire

00:01:08.680 --> 00:01:12.240
confident people. So, we chase

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:15.880
confidence. But that's the wrong move.

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:18.520
Serena Williams had won 23 Grand Slam

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:21.080
titles and spent two decades at the very

00:01:18.520 --> 00:01:23.920
top of her game. And still, she has been

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:26.160
open about this. She has struggles with

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:28.720
self-doubt. Same with Paul McCartney. He

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:30.800
is one of the most famous musicians on

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:32.760
the planet. But on a recent TV

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:35.280
interview, he just shrugged and said,

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:37.400
"Yeah, just like everyone else, you have

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:39.520
insecurities." So, we think confidence

00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:42.040
is waiting on the other side of our

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:44.600
success. But that's not where the real

00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:47.880
confidence comes from. In the late

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:51.120
1960s, a Stanford psychologist named

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:53.680
Albert Bandura worked with people who

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:58.320
were terrified of snakes. And not just

00:01:53.680 --> 00:02:02.160
afraid, but deep real phobia. And first,

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:05.200
they watched someone else hold a snake

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:08.920
from a safe distance. Then they took one

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:13.120
step closer. They touched the snake with

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:15.760
gloved hands. And then one step closer

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:18.800
without the gloves. And within a few

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:20.720
hours, the same people with serious

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:22.400
snake phobias were playing with the

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:23.959
snake in their lap. [music] That's

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:25.680
interesting enough, right? But what

00:02:23.959 --> 00:02:28.240
happened later was even more

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:30.920
fascinating. Their sense of confidence

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:34.040
did not stay in that room alone. Once

00:02:30.920 --> 00:02:36.840
those people had faced and overcome one

00:02:34.040 --> 00:02:38.511
fear, they started acting confidently in

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:38.560
many other parts of their lives.

00:02:38.511 --> 00:02:40.720
>> [music]

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:42.560
>> That's fascinating. They became more

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:44.120
willing to speak up. They stopped

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:46.440
feeling nervous about hard

00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:47.680
conversations. They took actions they

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:50.560
had been avoiding for a while.

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:52.520
Confidence built in one little corner of

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:54.480
your life can start spreading into other

00:02:52.520 --> 00:02:57.360
parts of your life. When you do a hard

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:59.120
thing and survive it, your brain files

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:01.120
it as a proof.

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:03.480
And that's how confidence starts getting

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:06.440
built [music] over time. And by the way,

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:09.040
I came across this snake story while I

00:03:06.440 --> 00:03:12.560
was researching for my newsletter. If

00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:15.360
you like ideas like this in your inbox,

00:03:12.560 --> 00:03:18.920
my newsletter goes deeper with one

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:20.280
insight, one tool, one [music] practice

00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:23.000
every Tuesday.

00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:24.080
Please subscribe. Link is below. And

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:26.120
it's [music] free. But what gets

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:28.800
developed here is not just your

00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:31.160
confidence. Something deeper starts

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:34.600
happening. What gets built is your

00:03:31.160 --> 00:03:36.959
self-trust. Because confidence is the

00:03:34.600 --> 00:03:39.519
feeling about the [music] outcome. I'll

00:03:36.959 --> 00:03:41.840
do well. That feeling can rise. Feeling

00:03:39.519 --> 00:03:44.360
can fall depending on what results you

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:47.400
get. Self-trust says, "Listen, I may not

00:03:44.360 --> 00:03:50.360
know the answers, but I trust myself to

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:53.480
figure it out." That is much stronger

00:03:50.360 --> 00:03:55.960
and it lasts longer. So, here's our

00:03:53.480 --> 00:03:58.680
first framework. I call it the proof

00:03:55.960 --> 00:04:00.560
pyramid. You need three kinds of proofs

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:03.760
to build that confidence, that

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:07.560
self-trust. The base of the pyramid is

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:09.640
proof of action. Do one small hard thing

00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:12.440
that you've been avoiding. The middle of

00:04:09.640 --> 00:04:14.680
the pyramid is proof of integrity. Make

00:04:12.440 --> 00:04:17.280
one small promise to yourself [music]

00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:19.359
and keep it every day. And finally, the

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:22.120
top of the pyramid is the proof of

00:04:19.359 --> 00:04:25.919
recovery. When you come back after

00:04:22.120 --> 00:04:28.720
failure or rejection or embarrassment,

00:04:25.919 --> 00:04:32.440
your brain gets rewired because

00:04:28.720 --> 00:04:34.840
self-trust only becomes real after we

00:04:32.440 --> 00:04:37.000
fail and recover. That's where we go

00:04:34.840 --> 00:04:40.280
next, [music] the important skill of

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:43.400
failing without becoming a failure. I

00:04:40.280 --> 00:04:46.720
have been fired three times in my career

00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:49.280
and none of that was gentle. One of them

00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:51.440
was so bizarre that I still remember it

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:53.320
vividly. So, I was serving as a chief

00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:56.360
revenue officer at this tech company in

00:04:53.320 --> 00:04:57.720
New York City and our new product was

00:04:56.360 --> 00:05:00.840
not doing well. It was delayed

00:04:57.720 --> 00:05:03.160
dramatically, our customers were very

00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:05.400
angry, they were leaving, revenue was

00:05:03.160 --> 00:05:07.560
tanking and there was a board meeting

00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:10.000
and after the board meeting, the CEO

00:05:07.560 --> 00:05:11.840
called me into the board room and he

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:13.200
said, "You know, we've decided to let

00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:15.560
you go.

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:16.640
I have to ask you to give me the access

00:05:15.560 --> 00:05:18.640
keys

00:05:16.640 --> 00:05:21.560
and don't go back to your office. We'll

00:05:18.640 --> 00:05:23.760
send you your things in boxes later."

00:05:21.560 --> 00:05:26.680
Literally, he walked me out to the

00:05:23.760 --> 00:05:28.840
elevator and waited there until I left

00:05:26.680 --> 00:05:32.320
and that was it. And on the train ride

00:05:28.840 --> 00:05:35.000
home, I just kept thinking about

00:05:32.320 --> 00:05:37.280
how I was going to tell my wife. What

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:41.040
would she think of me? What would I

00:05:37.280 --> 00:05:41.913
think of me? I was unemployed for almost

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:41.960
10 months.

00:05:41.913 --> 00:05:44.640
>> [music]

00:05:41.960 --> 00:05:47.000
>> I called recruiters, I had coffee with

00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:49.640
friends and former colleagues. I would

00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:51.520
ask everybody if they knew of any role,

00:05:49.640 --> 00:05:55.160
if any opening, [music]

00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:57.400
any consulting gig. Nothing for

00:05:55.160 --> 00:06:00.680
10 months, nothing. You know, the

00:05:57.400 --> 00:06:03.000
half-life of failure is very long, and

00:06:00.680 --> 00:06:06.480
it makes you feel small.

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:09.600
It makes you feel alone, but eventually

00:06:06.480 --> 00:06:12.080
I got hired again in a new role as a

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:14.080
chief strategy officer at an awesome

00:06:12.080 --> 00:06:17.284
company. And I got to work with a

00:06:14.080 --> 00:06:20.200
phenomenal CEO who became a great friend

00:06:17.284 --> 00:06:23.560
[music] and a mentor. And it became one

00:06:20.200 --> 00:06:25.560
of those stepping stones to my CEO role

00:06:23.560 --> 00:06:29.280
years [music] later. So, it works out in

00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:31.600
the end, but none of that was obvious to

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:34.440
me when I was hunting for a job for

00:06:31.600 --> 00:06:35.760
almost a year. And that is the problem

00:06:34.440 --> 00:06:38.720
with failure.

00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:41.640
It's a great teacher, but first it likes

00:06:38.720 --> 00:06:44.560
to punch you in the face. Now, our brain

00:06:41.640 --> 00:06:47.400
loves to explain failure in three ways.

00:06:44.560 --> 00:06:49.840
I am borrowing this from the works of a

00:06:47.400 --> 00:06:52.680
psychologist named Martin Seligman. You

00:06:49.840 --> 00:06:56.440
know, we see failure as either personal

00:06:52.680 --> 00:06:59.520
or permanent or pervasive, sometimes all

00:06:56.440 --> 00:07:01.720
three. First, we say, "My failure is

00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:04.640
personal. This clearly means that

00:07:01.720 --> 00:07:07.280
something is wrong with me." Second, "My

00:07:04.640 --> 00:07:10.160
failure is permanent. This will never

00:07:07.280 --> 00:07:13.120
change. I am doomed." And third is

00:07:10.160 --> 00:07:15.520
pervasive. I failed here, so I'll fail

00:07:13.120 --> 00:07:18.680
everywhere else. But once you see those

00:07:15.520 --> 00:07:21.000
patterns, you also realize that none of

00:07:18.680 --> 00:07:23.080
it is true. It's just that voice in your

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:25.400
head playing tricks on you. So, finally,

00:07:23.080 --> 00:07:27.520
let me share three cheat codes that

00:07:25.400 --> 00:07:29.720
helped me manage that mental model after

00:07:27.520 --> 00:07:32.560
failure. First, it's [music] actually

00:07:29.720 --> 00:07:34.720
good to make useful mistakes. Just feel

00:07:32.560 --> 00:07:37.360
comfortable about it. Not reckless

00:07:34.720 --> 00:07:40.000
mistakes, [music] but useful ones. If

00:07:37.360 --> 00:07:42.280
your rejection rate is too low, you're

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:44.080
you're swinging your bat enough times.

00:07:42.280 --> 00:07:47.080
Ed Sheeran is a great example. You know,

00:07:44.080 --> 00:07:48.640
he was rejected by every music label he

00:07:47.080 --> 00:07:51.960
approached. They thought he was too

00:07:48.640 --> 00:07:54.720
awkward, too acoustic, and too different

00:07:51.960 --> 00:07:57.280
looking to have that pop star persona.

00:07:54.720 --> 00:07:59.840
He played live and he would get booed.

00:07:57.280 --> 00:08:02.720
So what? He would go to the next town

00:07:59.840 --> 00:08:04.720
and he'd play again. Those were useful

00:08:02.720 --> 00:08:08.320
failures because he was gathering that

00:08:04.720 --> 00:08:11.400
feedback and refining his act. Now, he

00:08:08.320 --> 00:08:13.120
sells out stadiums as a one-man show and

00:08:11.400 --> 00:08:15.560
sings duets with Beyoncé. [music] The

00:08:13.120 --> 00:08:17.280
second cheat code is about portfolio

00:08:15.560 --> 00:08:19.560
thinking. So, all these venture

00:08:17.280 --> 00:08:21.160
capitalists know most of their bets

00:08:19.560 --> 00:08:24.040
might [music] fail and that's why they

00:08:21.160 --> 00:08:26.400
invest in so many of these companies so

00:08:24.040 --> 00:08:29.400
as to diversify their risk. You should

00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:32.280
think about risk and failure the same

00:08:29.400 --> 00:08:35.520
way. Take risks, but size them, limit

00:08:32.280 --> 00:08:38.280
them, diversify them so you can survive

00:08:35.520 --> 00:08:41.400
being wrong. And the third cheat code is

00:08:38.280 --> 00:08:44.200
the one none of us can cheat, death.

00:08:41.400 --> 00:08:46.360
Now, this may sound a bit too dramatic,

00:08:44.200 --> 00:08:49.360
but I got this advice from a mentor of

00:08:46.360 --> 00:08:51.960
mine. All of this ends the same way for

00:08:49.360 --> 00:08:54.560
all of us. That's the reality and it

00:08:51.960 --> 00:08:57.106
comforts me because it reminds me to

00:08:54.560 --> 00:08:57.800
focus on the things that truly matter

00:08:57.106 --> 00:09:00.400
[music]

00:08:57.800 --> 00:09:03.120
beyond our superficial definition of

00:09:00.400 --> 00:09:05.520
success and failure. So, you can

00:09:03.120 --> 00:09:07.640
implement any of these three cheat codes

00:09:05.520 --> 00:09:10.680
or all of them and that can change your

00:09:07.640 --> 00:09:13.839
relationship with failure. And you can

00:09:10.680 --> 00:09:15.839
fail often without feeling like you're a

00:09:13.839 --> 00:09:18.760
failure. Those are the two things you

00:09:15.839 --> 00:09:20.760
should learn to work on yourself. Now,

00:09:18.760 --> 00:09:22.520
let's go to the second level, working

00:09:20.760 --> 00:09:25.080
with others. And one of the most

00:09:22.520 --> 00:09:26.920
important skills there is selling. Life

00:09:25.080 --> 00:09:28.600
is about selling. Your first job

00:09:26.920 --> 00:09:30.440
interview, you have to sell. You're

00:09:28.600 --> 00:09:32.320
going on a date, you have to sell.

00:09:30.440 --> 00:09:34.400
Raising money for your company, you have

00:09:32.320 --> 00:09:37.040
to sell. Negotiating with your teenage

00:09:34.400 --> 00:09:39.080
daughter to get her off Instagram, you

00:09:37.040 --> 00:09:41.160
have to sell. Maybe that's the hardest

00:09:39.080 --> 00:09:43.440
one because Instagram has sold her

00:09:41.160 --> 00:09:44.921
something that is way more powerful. And

00:09:43.440 --> 00:09:45.000
that's because selling

00:09:44.921 --> 00:09:46.960
>> [music]

00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:49.160
>> is about influence. The biggest

00:09:46.960 --> 00:09:51.960
misconception about selling is that it's

00:09:49.160 --> 00:09:55.440
about tactics. It's not. It's about

00:09:51.960 --> 00:09:58.960
credibility. The best salespeople I know

00:09:55.440 --> 00:10:01.640
spend years becoming believable. And

00:09:58.960 --> 00:10:04.080
then they simply say what they believe

00:10:01.640 --> 00:10:06.000
in. And that's what closes the deal.

00:10:04.080 --> 00:10:09.400
That's what influences people. You know,

00:10:06.000 --> 00:10:13.320
humans are hardwired to resist being

00:10:09.400 --> 00:10:15.920
sold to. The moment you sense a trick,

00:10:13.320 --> 00:10:17.560
you'll realize your defenses go up

00:10:15.920 --> 00:10:19.680
because people don't want to buy

00:10:17.560 --> 00:10:21.600
products. They want to buy better

00:10:19.680 --> 00:10:23.400
versions of themselves. [music]

00:10:21.600 --> 00:10:26.280
That's the transformation. That's the

00:10:23.400 --> 00:10:30.160
influence. And that's why Nike ads would

00:10:26.280 --> 00:10:32.360
rarely mention the shoe. Same for Apple,

00:10:30.160 --> 00:10:34.720
same for Harley-Davidson, same for

00:10:32.360 --> 00:10:37.080
Patagonia. They don't sell products.

00:10:34.720 --> 00:10:39.600
They sell feelings. So that's what

00:10:37.080 --> 00:10:41.880
selling is about. And the simplest way

00:10:39.600 --> 00:10:43.920
of thinking about four skills that

00:10:41.880 --> 00:10:46.200
you're going to need to become a very

00:10:43.920 --> 00:10:50.520
good seller is a framework that I call

00:10:46.200 --> 00:10:52.600
care. C A R E, care. Because that's

00:10:50.520 --> 00:10:56.360
literally what it takes to [music] sell,

00:10:52.600 --> 00:10:58.280
to influence. C is curiosity. Focus on

00:10:56.360 --> 00:11:00.360
what others want. When someone wants a

00:10:58.280 --> 00:11:02.480
promotion, for example, they don't just

00:11:00.360 --> 00:11:04.680
want promotion. They want recognition.

00:11:02.480 --> 00:11:07.320
They want financial [music] safety. And

00:11:04.680 --> 00:11:09.800
you won't get to that truth unless

00:11:07.320 --> 00:11:12.640
you're genuinely curious. A is

00:11:09.800 --> 00:11:15.160
alignment. Now, alignment is about

00:11:12.640 --> 00:11:17.839
connecting the dots between what you

00:11:15.160 --> 00:11:21.160
have and what the other person wants.

00:11:17.839 --> 00:11:23.960
That matching requires a lot of empathy.

00:11:21.160 --> 00:11:26.080
R is reliability. Just tell the truth.

00:11:23.960 --> 00:11:27.320
You don't have to sell. Tell them what

00:11:26.080 --> 00:11:29.400
you believe in.

00:11:27.320 --> 00:11:32.280
Tell them why you believe in it. Tell

00:11:29.400 --> 00:11:34.640
them what your product cannot do or else

00:11:32.280 --> 00:11:37.720
they won't believe what it can do.

00:11:34.640 --> 00:11:40.520
So, trust takes years to build and

00:11:37.720 --> 00:11:43.600
seconds to destroy. And finally, E for

00:11:40.520 --> 00:11:46.280
emotion. Make them feel the future

00:11:43.600 --> 00:11:49.040
version of themselves. Masters like

00:11:46.280 --> 00:11:51.160
Steve Jobs understood this reality when

00:11:49.040 --> 00:11:52.920
they said, "A thousand songs in your

00:11:51.160 --> 00:11:55.560
pocket." He didn't talk about the

00:11:52.920 --> 00:11:58.040
storage limits or gigabytes or any of

00:11:55.560 --> 00:12:00.760
that technology stuff because he knew

00:11:58.040 --> 00:12:02.720
that facts will make you nod, but

00:12:00.760 --> 00:12:05.920
feelings will make you [music] move. And

00:12:02.720 --> 00:12:07.920
moving people is what selling is about.

00:12:05.920 --> 00:12:10.960
And that takes us to the second skill

00:12:07.920 --> 00:12:13.000
you need, how to build others. The most

00:12:10.960 --> 00:12:15.960
valuable person in the history of

00:12:13.000 --> 00:12:18.440
Silicon Valley had no formal power. Bill

00:12:15.960 --> 00:12:21.120
Campbell was a football coach who got

00:12:18.440 --> 00:12:23.640
fired at the age of 39. But then he went

00:12:21.120 --> 00:12:26.400
on to become a mentor to founders and

00:12:23.640 --> 00:12:28.240
companies that have defined our entire

00:12:26.400 --> 00:12:31.880
digital lives in more ways than one.

00:12:28.240 --> 00:12:34.800
Google, Apple, Amazon, Meta, Twitter,

00:12:31.880 --> 00:12:37.360
eBay, Yahoo, Intuit. His impact on

00:12:34.800 --> 00:12:39.360
Silicon Valley was like no other. The

00:12:37.360 --> 00:12:41.000
interesting part about Bill was that he

00:12:39.360 --> 00:12:43.640
didn't know how to build software, but

00:12:41.000 --> 00:12:47.200
he knew how to build people. A long time

00:12:43.640 --> 00:12:49.360
ago, I knew a CEO who was one of these

00:12:47.200 --> 00:12:51.520
genius types. You know, he had a great

00:12:49.360 --> 00:12:53.839
team and he was building a company that

00:12:51.520 --> 00:12:56.800
was absolutely killing in the market. He

00:12:53.839 --> 00:12:58.920
was unquestionably the smartest person

00:12:56.800 --> 00:13:00.800
in the room and he knew how to build a

00:12:58.920 --> 00:13:02.880
company, but he was not very good at

00:13:00.800 --> 00:13:06.160
building people. He was able to deliver

00:13:02.880 --> 00:13:09.000
revenue numbers that were huge, but he

00:13:06.160 --> 00:13:10.600
also made his people feel small.

00:13:09.000 --> 00:13:13.080
Eventually, the company's [music] stock

00:13:10.600 --> 00:13:15.080
plummeted, the executives around him

00:13:13.080 --> 00:13:17.880
started leaving one by one. You know,

00:13:15.080 --> 00:13:20.960
you can have a great mission, a great

00:13:17.880 --> 00:13:23.240
balance sheet, but if your people don't

00:13:20.960 --> 00:13:27.320
feel valued, you'll lose all three of

00:13:23.240 --> 00:13:29.360
them. Google ran an experiment on 180

00:13:27.320 --> 00:13:31.800
internal teams to find out what do the

00:13:29.360 --> 00:13:34.080
best teams have in common. And the

00:13:31.800 --> 00:13:36.440
answer was surprising.

00:13:34.080 --> 00:13:38.800
The best teams were not the ones with

00:13:36.440 --> 00:13:40.960
the highest IQs. They were the ones

00:13:38.800 --> 00:13:43.200
where people could speak their mind.

00:13:40.960 --> 00:13:46.000
It's called psychological safety. Teams

00:13:43.200 --> 00:13:49.400
that felt safe delivered way more than

00:13:46.000 --> 00:13:52.320
teams with much higher raw IQ. That's

00:13:49.400 --> 00:13:54.560
what otherness is about. It has nothing

00:13:52.320 --> 00:13:57.680
to do with being nice. It is one of the

00:13:54.560 --> 00:13:59.120
most important career moves in your

00:13:57.680 --> 00:14:01.240
life. So, how do you create

00:13:59.120 --> 00:14:03.920
psychological safety? How do you focus

00:14:01.240 --> 00:14:06.440
on others? By giving three things away.

00:14:03.920 --> 00:14:09.040
First, give attention. The rarest

00:14:06.440 --> 00:14:11.640
resource anyone has nowadays is

00:14:09.040 --> 00:14:14.080
attention. So, when you focus on someone

00:14:11.640 --> 00:14:17.040
fully, even for a minute, you can make

00:14:14.080 --> 00:14:20.160
magic happen. Make the other person feel

00:14:17.040 --> 00:14:22.400
fully seen. Second, give credit. When

00:14:20.160 --> 00:14:25.400
things go well, find the person who is

00:14:22.400 --> 00:14:27.960
responsible and just thank them. And you

00:14:25.400 --> 00:14:30.280
can be specific about exactly what it

00:14:27.960 --> 00:14:33.000
was that you appreciate about their

00:14:30.280 --> 00:14:35.240
effort, about their talents, about their

00:14:33.000 --> 00:14:37.080
judgment, whatever it is. Name that

00:14:35.240 --> 00:14:38.800
specific thing

00:14:37.080 --> 00:14:41.560
that they did well. When you remind

00:14:38.800 --> 00:14:43.720
people about how good they are, they

00:14:41.560 --> 00:14:46.360
will remember how great you [music]

00:14:43.720 --> 00:14:49.040
were. And finally, give ownership. This

00:14:46.360 --> 00:14:51.600
one grows with you. If you're in a team,

00:14:49.040 --> 00:14:53.400
this is about letting your teammate

00:14:51.600 --> 00:14:55.000
present the work that you guys have done

00:14:53.400 --> 00:14:56.800
together. If you're a manager, it's

00:14:55.000 --> 00:14:58.720
about letting the team own their

00:14:56.800 --> 00:15:01.080
outcomes. Make them feel like they're

00:14:58.720 --> 00:15:03.160
owners, not helpers. These three can

00:15:01.080 --> 00:15:05.560
change the entire trajectory of people

00:15:03.160 --> 00:15:08.080
you work with. And when they grow,

00:15:05.560 --> 00:15:10.680
you grow faster. So far, we have talked

00:15:08.080 --> 00:15:12.880
about your relationship with yourself

00:15:10.680 --> 00:15:15.045
and with others. Now, let's get to the

00:15:12.880 --> 00:15:15.839
third part, dealing with reality.

00:15:15.045 --> 00:15:18.480
[music]

00:15:15.839 --> 00:15:21.320
Let's start by dealing with money.

00:15:18.480 --> 00:15:24.870
Warren Buffett is 94 years old, and

00:15:21.320 --> 00:15:27.160
there's a number nobody talks about. 98%

00:15:24.870 --> 00:15:30.765
[music] of Warren Buffett's wealth came

00:15:27.160 --> 00:15:35.000
after his 65th birthday. At 65, he was

00:15:30.765 --> 00:15:36.920
[music] worth 3 billion. Today, it's 160

00:15:35.000 --> 00:15:39.600
billion. [music] And he says it himself,

00:15:36.920 --> 00:15:42.360
"My life has been a product of compound

00:15:39.600 --> 00:15:45.520
interest." The greatest investor alive

00:15:42.360 --> 00:15:47.640
made almost all his money after the age

00:15:45.520 --> 00:15:50.080
where most of us retire. That's what our

00:15:47.640 --> 00:15:52.120
brains just don't understand. We're

00:15:50.080 --> 00:15:54.240
incapable of understanding the idea of

00:15:52.120 --> 00:15:55.800
compounding. And that's why it's one of

00:15:54.240 --> 00:15:57.400
those things that most of us don't

00:15:55.800 --> 00:15:59.560
understand about [music] money.

00:15:57.400 --> 00:16:01.840
Financial literacy is a skill that helps

00:15:59.560 --> 00:16:04.120
you deal with that kind of reality. But

00:16:01.840 --> 00:16:06.720
it's not as widespread a skill as you

00:16:04.120 --> 00:16:09.560
would think. In 2021, two economists

00:16:06.720 --> 00:16:12.320
wrote three simple questions to measure

00:16:09.560 --> 00:16:15.360
whether a person understands how money

00:16:12.320 --> 00:16:19.240
works. One, if you have $100 at [music]

00:16:15.360 --> 00:16:22.680
2% interest, do you have more or less

00:16:19.240 --> 00:16:27.751
than $102 after 5 years? Two, if your

00:16:22.680 --> 00:16:27.880
savings earn 1% and inflation is at 2%,

00:16:27.751 --> 00:16:30.680
>> [music]

00:16:27.880 --> 00:16:33.760
>> can you buy more or less a year from

00:16:30.680 --> 00:16:35.960
now? And three, is a single stock safer

00:16:33.760 --> 00:16:38.000
than a mutual fund? That's it. Those

00:16:35.960 --> 00:16:38.886
were the three basic questions. That was

00:16:38.000 --> 00:16:39.360
the whole exam.

00:16:38.886 --> 00:16:43.640
>> [music]

00:16:39.360 --> 00:16:46.000
>> But still, in the US, more than 85% of

00:16:43.640 --> 00:16:47.680
adults under the age of 35 [music]

00:16:46.000 --> 00:16:50.320
got at least one of them wrong. And

00:16:47.680 --> 00:16:52.280
finance is one of the strongest forces

00:16:50.320 --> 00:16:54.920
that will shape the rest of their lives.

00:16:52.280 --> 00:16:56.400
This is the most expensive ignorance

00:16:54.920 --> 00:16:57.960
they can't afford to have. And

00:16:56.400 --> 00:16:59.880
underneath those questions, the

00:16:57.960 --> 00:17:02.320
researchers [music] wanted all of us to

00:16:59.880 --> 00:17:04.600
understand three realities about money.

00:17:02.320 --> 00:17:07.720
First, money compounds over the long

00:17:04.600 --> 00:17:10.079
term. So, be patient. Second, inflation

00:17:07.720 --> 00:17:12.360
will always eat into your money unless

00:17:10.079 --> 00:17:14.160
you invest in a way that keeps you ahead

00:17:12.360 --> 00:17:18.186
of the inflation rate. And third,

00:17:14.160 --> 00:17:19.640
diversification is always always better

00:17:18.186 --> 00:17:21.600
[music] than concentration. So, that's

00:17:19.640 --> 00:17:23.760
how you deal with the reality of money.

00:17:21.600 --> 00:17:25.240
And now, let's go to the sixth skill

00:17:23.760 --> 00:17:28.600
about how to deal with reality,

00:17:25.240 --> 00:17:32.040
judgment. We build our entire careers,

00:17:28.600 --> 00:17:34.600
status, and dreams upon one thing, and

00:17:32.040 --> 00:17:36.640
AI made that into a commodity. Think

00:17:34.600 --> 00:17:38.520
about all that we've coveted in our

00:17:36.640 --> 00:17:41.320
lives. We try to get into the best

00:17:38.520 --> 00:17:43.400
universities that we can, we find jobs

00:17:41.320 --> 00:17:46.160
at the most prestigious companies that

00:17:43.400 --> 00:17:48.240
we can, we try to spend time learning

00:17:46.160 --> 00:17:51.640
and teaching and growing. [music] And

00:17:48.240 --> 00:17:54.520
now comes AI and it commoditizes the one

00:17:51.640 --> 00:17:57.640
thing that we relied upon the most,

00:17:54.520 --> 00:18:00.600
intelligence. So, what can you do in an

00:17:57.640 --> 00:18:03.760
era where machines can outthink you?

00:18:00.600 --> 00:18:05.480
Well, you have to outjudge the machines.

00:18:03.760 --> 00:18:07.840
If you're trying to write an essay, for

00:18:05.480 --> 00:18:10.480
example, AI will generate millions of

00:18:07.840 --> 00:18:11.242
lines in just a few minutes. The real

00:18:10.480 --> 00:18:11.480
question is,

00:18:11.242 --> 00:18:14.000
>> [music]

00:18:11.480 --> 00:18:16.520
>> which lines are you going to pick? Your

00:18:14.000 --> 00:18:18.160
judgment and your taste are the skills

00:18:16.520 --> 00:18:20.440
that make all the difference in this

00:18:18.160 --> 00:18:23.240
era. And here the main three challenges

00:18:20.440 --> 00:18:26.200
using AI without judgment. First, AI can

00:18:23.240 --> 00:18:28.160
fake deep fluency. It sounds right when

00:18:26.200 --> 00:18:29.840
it's wrong. Second, AI is going to drive

00:18:28.160 --> 00:18:32.920
you to the average. It gives you the

00:18:29.840 --> 00:18:34.640
most average of what everyone thinks.

00:18:32.920 --> 00:18:36.920
There's no way to differentiate

00:18:34.640 --> 00:18:39.720
yourself. And third, AI seeks

00:18:36.920 --> 00:18:42.840
confirmation. It wants to please you so

00:18:39.720 --> 00:18:44.647
desperately all the time at any cost, so

00:18:42.840 --> 00:18:46.320
it always agrees with you all the time.

00:18:44.647 --> 00:18:47.920
[music] And the only way to protect

00:18:46.320 --> 00:18:50.520
yourself from all those three

00:18:47.920 --> 00:18:52.640
challenges, your judgment. I have a

00:18:50.520 --> 00:18:53.800
separate video that I made that goes

00:18:52.640 --> 00:18:56.280
deep into [music]

00:18:53.800 --> 00:18:58.800
the idea of judgment and critical

00:18:56.280 --> 00:19:00.840
thinking, how to develop it, how to

00:18:58.800 --> 00:19:03.240
protect it, how [music] to nurture it,

00:19:00.840 --> 00:19:05.640
because I think this will be one of the

00:19:03.240 --> 00:19:07.840
most important skills to develop in the

00:19:05.640 --> 00:19:10.320
age of AI. [music] Now, when you try to

00:19:07.840 --> 00:19:13.080
make a decision, when should you trust

00:19:10.320 --> 00:19:15.240
your gut feeling? The answer is

00:19:13.080 --> 00:19:18.120
surprisingly simple. Trust it when you

00:19:15.240 --> 00:19:20.760
can get fast, honest feedback on your

00:19:18.120 --> 00:19:23.400
decisions. A firefighter is able to

00:19:20.760 --> 00:19:25.680
build real judgment because fire gives

00:19:23.400 --> 00:19:28.520
feedback to him in real time. The more

00:19:25.680 --> 00:19:31.153
reps you have with clear and quick

00:19:28.520 --> 00:19:33.280
feedback, the better your judgment gets

00:19:31.153 --> 00:19:36.880
[music] over time. So, those are the six

00:19:33.280 --> 00:19:38.640
skills. Now, every single skill in this

00:19:36.880 --> 00:19:40.840
video has one thing in common. [music]

00:19:38.640 --> 00:19:43.240
They're all like restless chickens

00:19:40.840 --> 00:19:46.000
running around in your barn. You have to

00:19:43.240 --> 00:19:48.920
constantly keep chasing them. Otherwise,

00:19:46.000 --> 00:19:52.680
they'll run away from you. So, the most

00:19:48.920 --> 00:19:54.960
important meta-skill is learning how to

00:19:52.680 --> 00:19:58.080
learn these skills. The world that is

00:19:54.960 --> 00:20:00.440
coming our way will require us to pivot

00:19:58.080 --> 00:20:02.760
so many times because the hills you want

00:20:00.440 --> 00:20:04.440
to climb will change their shapes right

00:20:02.760 --> 00:20:06.600
under your feet. While you're busy

00:20:04.440 --> 00:20:08.760
studying the map, you cannot afford to

00:20:06.600 --> 00:20:11.120
get rusty on any of these skills that

00:20:08.760 --> 00:20:14.080
we've covered. [music] And I see this as

00:20:11.120 --> 00:20:17.440
a great gift. I think there's a certain

00:20:14.080 --> 00:20:20.360
amount of joy in being a student again.

00:20:17.440 --> 00:20:22.720
Empty your cup once again, so a fresh

00:20:20.360 --> 00:20:25.760
brew can fill it. You know, I always

00:20:22.720 --> 00:20:27.160
remember a lesson my teacher taught me a

00:20:25.760 --> 00:20:30.240
long time ago.

00:20:27.160 --> 00:20:33.520
Some things cannot be taught.

00:20:30.240 --> 00:20:34.640
They must be remembered.

00:20:33.520 --> 00:20:38.760
The most important [music]

00:20:34.640 --> 00:20:42.600
things in your life, your judgment, your

00:20:38.760 --> 00:20:45.240
empathy, your genuine character,

00:20:42.600 --> 00:20:49.840
cannot be handed to you by a [music]

00:20:45.240 --> 00:20:53.280
teacher or a book or a guide.

00:20:49.840 --> 00:20:56.440
You have to quiet the noise so they can

00:20:53.280 --> 00:20:59.520
rise to the surface.

00:20:56.440 --> 00:21:02.000
Nobody can give you what's already

00:20:59.520 --> 00:21:03.600
yours.

00:21:02.000 --> 00:21:04.600
I'll see you next week.

00:21:03.600 --> 00:21:07.280
Thank you.

00:21:04.600 --> 00:21:07.280
And I love you.

